    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so if the capacitor is \"storing\" charge during the AC cycle, how does that actually look in terms of the current flow? like is the current actually moving through the capacitor or is it just building up on the plates?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In an AC circuit, the apparent movement of charge in the capacitor is an illusion. There is no actual current flowing through the capacitor, because the direction of the electric field changes as the capacitor charges and discharges. Instead, the electric field and magnetic field created by the changing charge in the capacitor cause the current to flow in the surrounding circuit, a phenomenon known as capacitance-induced current. This current is not directly flowing through the capacitor plates.",
      "source": "target",
