    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so the charge just stays on the plates and moves back and forth. i'm finding it hard to visualize why the frequency matters so much though. like, why does a higher frequency result in lower reactance? can u explain the relationship between the frequency of the AC and the reactance in a bit more detail?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The frequency of an AC circuit determines the time rate at which the capacitor can charge or discharge. At higher frequencies, the plates of the capacitor cannot fully charge and discharge in one cycle, resulting in less movement of charge. This reduced movement of charge means the capacitor has a harder time opposing the change in voltage, resulting in lower capacitive reactance. Think of it like trying to close a door quickly: if the door opens and closes really fast, it's harder to close it when it opens, whereas if it opens and closes slowly, it's easier to close it when it opens.",
      "source": "target",
